      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-142112",
        "knowledge_point_id": "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form",
        "problem": "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x+2y\\le 4?$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & \\le 4 \\\\ 2y & \\le -x+4 \\\\ y & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**. \n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/8b906cf167528f484109528a93732dc2b8d73558bb02f454bb520af87efde3c5.png)",
