        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable",
        "problem": "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\nII. It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n\nIII. It is differentiable at $x=0$",
        "worked_solution": "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there.",
