        "knowledge_point_id": "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a",
        "knowledge_point": "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions",
        "problem": "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
